  "name": "Kiwi & Moa × I-1 — Kamimbo Bay",
  "description": "Night of 29–30 Jan 1943 off Kamimbo Bay, northwest Guadalcanal. Opens on HMNZS Kiwi's asdic contact at 3,000 yd (clock 21:05): Lt Cdr Gordon Bridson's Bird-class minesweeper has turned in on Junsen Type J1 submarine I-1 (Lt Cdr Eiichi Sakamoto), submerged at 30 m on a supply run for the 17th Army, and is working up to 13 kn for a depth-charge attack. HMNZS Moa (Lt Cdr Peter Phipps) is 1 nm east, still steering west on patrol as the directing ship. The reef where I-1 grounded is 09°13′S 159°40′E, 0.9 nm due south of her start. Seats: Kiwi password kiwi token kiwi-demo; Moa password moa token moa-demo; I-1 password red token i1-demo; umpire umpire. Figures are minesweeper and cruiser-submarine stats on Destroyer and Fleet Submarine seats (13 kn, 51.2 m; 18 kn surfaced, 97.5 m; tubes seeded 4 bow + 2 stern; asdic 2.5 nm). The fleet-sub seat installs a radar set I-1 did not carry; it is cut to 1 nm and is dead while she is at 30 m. 21:05 is night for the optics sky (lookout range is unchanged). Published clocks for the grounding disagree (about 20:40 and 23:20). A ram registers only when I-1's keel is at or above 8 m and a bow is within 35° of the other hull; the reef itself is not modeled, so the umpire beaches her if she reaches 09°13′S 159°40′E. Star shell is not fired by the engine.",
